  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Winds start to diminish through early evening. \n\nNot getting as many gusts over 40 mph as earlier, and will\nlikely see winds abate after 4pm, and gusts drop off moreso at\nsunset. Will let the wind advisory expire at 4pm.\n\nKey Message 2: Some freeze/frost late tonight in WV. Better\ncoverage of sub-freezing temps Monday night for most of the\narea.\n\nWinds are going to stay elevated enough tonight despite the cold\nadvection to limit frost formation. Still sub-freezing temps are\nlikely in western Greenbrier County. Although am not seeing much\nfrost potential given winds, plus a disturbance moving across\nwith clouds and a a few snowflakes, will not cancel the frost\nadvisory. A few sheltered spots are dawn could get some frost.\n\nWith the high overhead Monday night, models are still a little\non the milder side, but given light/calm winds and very dry\nairmass, went a few degrees colder so much of VA, southeast WV\nand the mountains of NC should reach at or below freezing late\nMonday night. A frost also possible, though airmass is dry for\nthe NC piedmont.\n\nKey Message 3. Warming up through the week with not much shower\nchances until next weekend.\n\nUpper trough lifts out midweek with temps returning to above\nnormal, but not looking quite as warm as last week, but still\nhighs in the 70s in the mountains and lower to mid 80s in the\nPiedmont are going to be 10-15 degrees above normal.\n\nShould see a few showers/storms late in the week with southwest\nflow and some increase in moisture, though will have to wait til\na front approaches this weekend for a better chance for showers.",
